  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Holomorphic Functions and Cauchy-Riemann [S+N]\n\nA function $f : \\Omega \\to \\mathbb{C}$ on an open set $\\Omega \\subseteq \\mathbb{C}$ is **complex differentiable at $z_0$** if\n\n$$f'(z_0) = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{f(z_0 + h) - f(z_0)}{h}$$\n\nexists, *with $h \\in \\mathbb{C}$ approaching $0$ from any direction*. When this holds at every point of $\\Omega$, $f$ is **holomorphic on $\\Omega$**.\n\nThis is a **much stronger** condition than $\\mathbb{R}^2$-differentiability of $f = u + iv$ as a map $\\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$. The extra rigidity is captured by the **Cauchy-Riemann equations**:\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}, \\qquad \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = -\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}.$$\n\nIf $u, v$ are $C^1$ and satisfy CR, then $f = u + iv$ is holomorphic. The converse holds too.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 6,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Why CR? Direction-independence of the limit\n\nApproach $h = 0$ along the real axis: $h = h_x \\to 0$. The difference quotient is\n\n$$\\frac{f(z + h_x) - f(z)}{h_x} \\to \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} + i\\,\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}.$$\n\nApproach along the imaginary axis: $h = i h_y \\to 0$. The quotient is\n\n$$\\frac{f(z + i h_y) - f(z)}{i h_y} \\to \\frac{1}{i}\\left(\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} + i\\,\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\right) = -i \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} + \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}.$$\n\nEquating real and imaginary parts gives the CR equations. **Both directions must yield the same limit** — that's what \"complex differentiable\" means.\n\n## Verifying CR for $f(z) = z^2$ [S+N]\n\n$z^2 = (x + iy)^2 = (x^2 - y^2) + i(2xy)$, so $u = x^2 - y^2$, $v = 2xy$.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "u : x^2 - y^2$\nv : 2*x*y$\nprint(\"∂u/∂x =\", diff(u, x), \"  ∂v/∂y =\", diff(v, y))$\nprint(\"∂u/∂y =\", diff(u, y), \"  -∂v/∂x =\", -diff(v, x))$\nprint(\"CR holds:\", is(equal(diff(u,x), diff(v,y))) and is(equal(diff(u,y), -diff(v,x))))$\nprint()$\nprint(\"f'(z) = ∂u/∂x + i ∂v/∂x =\", diff(u, x), \"+\", diff(v, x), \"i = 2(x + iy) = 2z ✓\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 7,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "∂u/∂x = 2*x   ∂v/∂y = 2*x\n∂u/∂y = -(2*y)   -∂v/∂x = -(2*y)\nCR holds: true\nf'(z) = ∂u/∂x + i ∂v/∂x = 2*x + 2*y i = 2(x + iy) = 2z ✓"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## A function that is *not* holomorphic: $f(z) = \\bar z$\n\nConjugation $\\bar z = x - iy$ has $u = x$, $v = -y$. Then $\\partial u/\\partial x = 1$ but $\\partial v/\\partial y = -1$ — CR fails.\n\nSo $\\bar z$ is **not** complex differentiable anywhere. It's perfectly $\\mathbb{R}^2$-differentiable as a map (just a reflection!), but the complex derivative doesn't exist because the limit depends on direction.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "u_conj : x$  v_conj : -y$\nprint(\"For f(z) = z̄: ∂u/∂x =\", diff(u_conj, x), \"  ∂v/∂y =\", diff(v_conj, y))$\nprint(\"CR fails — z̄ is not holomorphic, anywhere.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 8,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "For f(z) = z̄: ∂u/∂x = 1   ∂v/∂y = -1\nCR fails — z̄ is not holomorphic, anywhere."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## CR ⇔ \"no $\\bar z$ in the formula\"\n\nHeuristic: a holomorphic function is one that can be written **purely in terms of $z$**, with no $\\bar z$ appearing. Anywhere $\\bar z$ shows up, CR breaks.\n\nExamples:\n- $f(z) = z^2$ — holomorphic.\n- $f(z) = e^z$ — holomorphic.\n- $f(z) = \\sin z, \\cos z$ — holomorphic (extend Taylor series to $\\mathbb{C}$).\n- $f(z) = |z|^2 = z \\bar z$ — **not** holomorphic (uses $\\bar z$).\n- $f(z) = \\mathrm{Re}\\,z = (z + \\bar z)/2$ — not holomorphic.\n\n## Harmonic real and imaginary parts\n\nIf $f = u + iv$ is holomorphic, then both $u$ and $v$ satisfy **Laplace's equation**:\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial x^2} + \\frac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial y^2} = 0$$\n\n(and similarly for $v$). Functions satisfying Laplace's equation are called **harmonic**. So holomorphic functions are pairs of harmonic functions whose level sets are orthogonal — extraordinarily structured.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "u_check : x^2 - y^2$\nlaplacian_u : diff(u_check, x, 2) + diff(u_check, y, 2)$\nprint(\"Δu for u = x² - y²:\", laplacian_u)$\nprint(\"→ harmonic ✓\")$\nprint()$\nv_check : 2*x*y$\nlaplacian_v : diff(v_check, x, 2) + diff(v_check, y, 2)$\nprint(\"Δv for v = 2xy:\", laplacian_v)$\nprint(\"→ harmonic ✓\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 9,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Δu for u = x² - y²: 0\n→ harmonic ✓\nΔv for v = 2xy: 0\n→ harmonic ✓"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Visualising holomorphic mapping\n\nPlot grid lines under $f(z) = z^2$. The squaring doubles arguments and squares moduli, so a square grid in the right half-plane maps to a parabolic family.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Grid of horizontal/vertical lines in the strip 0 < Re z < 1.5, -1 < Im z < 1 */\nn_pts : 80$\nxs : np_to_list(np_linspace(0.1, 1.5, n_pts))$\nys : np_to_list(np_linspace(-1, 1, n_pts))$\n\n/* Vertical line at x = 0.5: image under z² = (x²-y²) + i(2xy) at fixed x */\nout_x_v_re : map(lambda([yy], 0.5*0.5 - yy*yy), ys)$\nout_x_v_im : map(lambda([yy], 2*0.5*yy), ys)$\nout_x_h_re : map(lambda([yy], 1.0*1.0 - yy*yy), ys)$\nout_x_h_im : map(lambda([yy], 2*1.0*yy), ys)$\nout_y_h_re : map(lambda([xx], xx*xx - 0.5*0.5), xs)$\nout_y_h_im : map(lambda([xx], 2*xx*0.5), xs)$\n\nax_draw2d(\n  color=\"blue\", line_width=2, name=\"image of x = 0.5\",\n  lines(out_x_v_re, out_x_v_im),\n  color=\"green\", line_width=2, name=\"image of x = 1.0\",\n  lines(out_x_h_re, out_x_h_im),\n  color=\"red\", line_width=2, name=\"image of y = 0.5\",\n  lines(out_y_h_re, out_y_h_im),\n  title=\"Image of grid lines under z² — parabolas\",\n  xlabel=\"Re\", ylabel=\"Im\",\n  grid=true, aspect_ratio=true, showlegend=true\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 10,
      "outputs": [
