      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "**Conformality**: holomorphic functions with $f'(z) \\ne 0$ preserve angles. The intersecting grid lines stay perpendicular after transformation. This is a geometric consequence of CR — and underpins the conformal-mapping notebook (#7).\n\n## Why holomorphy is so much stronger than $C^\\infty$\n\nThree foreshadowing facts (proven in coming notebooks):\n\n- **Holomorphic ⇒ $C^\\infty$**: a single complex derivative implies infinitely many.\n- **Holomorphic ⇒ analytic**: locally, $f$ equals its Taylor series.\n- **Identity theorem**: if $f, g$ are holomorphic and agree on a set with a limit point, they're equal everywhere on the connected component.\n\nNone of these has a real analogue. Smooth real functions are *much* more permissive; holomorphic complex functions are bound by an extraordinary internal coherence.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}